More than at other listening platforms, Seton appears interested in finding ways for SoundCloud to work with licensed AI music companies. In the past two weeks, SoundCloud has inked deals with Moises and ElevenLabs’ Eleven Music so that users can easily port over their AI-aided creations to SoundCloud and vice versa. These deals add to the platform’s 10 other AI music partnerships dating back to 2024 , including with Tuney, Tuttii, AIBeatz, TwoShot, Starmony and ACE Studio.

While Spotify has also eyed new opportunities with AI music, it has focused on developing its own AI-powered music remixing tool in-app, which is expected to debut early next year, rather than providing a pipeline to trusted partners.&nbsp;

SoundCloud boasts most songs that are available on other streaming platforms, but it fulfills a different purpose for many of its users. Seton says that 85% of listening on the platform today is to user-generated content (UGC), including remixes, covers and demos. Its DIY ethos makes it, perhaps, a more fertile ground for sharing AI-enabled songs. In fact, it already has been: One of the biggest AI music moments to date took place on SoundCloud when Metro Boomin uploaded “BBL Drizzy,” his remix of an AI song by Udio user King Willonius, in an effort to get back at Drake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

In a new open letter , published exclusively with Billboard, Seton explains the company’s stance on AI today, writing that “the future of music should be artist-first” and that AI music is allowed as long as it does not violate the platform’s new community guidelines and terms of use about “spam, fraud and deceptive activity.”&nbsp;

“AI slop has no place on SoundCloud,” Seton’s letter reads, adding that “SoundCloud will not tolerate deceptive use of AI to manipulate platform systems, exploit creator opportunities or mislead artists and fans.”

In the past, SoundCloud’s mostly open-minded approach to AI music has led to controversy. In February 2024, the platform quietly changed its terms of use, adding language that users “explicitly agree that your content may be used to inform, train, develop or serve as input to artificial intelligence or machine intelligence technologies or services as part of and for providing the services.” While that policy flew under the radar for about a year, it went viral in 2025, prompting outrage online and a quick backpedal from Seton. The company quickly removed the policy, and Seton wrote an open letter admitting that the policy created too much confusion and that the service never used anyone’s content to train generative AI models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

How Each Music Listening Platform Is Treating AI Music&nbsp;(Updating)

Now, in a new interview with Billboard, Seton opens up about his new policies against slop and fraud, and why he thinks the conversation around AI should be a continual dialogue. “Given how quickly the world is transforming, no one can predict the future,” Seton adds. “I think what we can do, rather than trying to hit moving targets, is establish a set of principles and values that will guide how we work. For us, that means being artist-first.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

You’ve announced a few partnerships with AI music companies over the last couple of weeks. Now, users of ElevenLabs’ Eleven Music and Moises are able to publish their works to SoundCloud directly. Why did you want to partner with these two companies?&nbsp;

This is a moment where we intend to help drive this debate and dialogue forward in the industry, because there’s been a very binary frame: It is either good or evil, accept it or reject it. AI music has been framed that way. As more of the commercial and legal frameworks are being established, it’s high time we changed the frame from something so binary and reductive to include the nuance that artists deserve.&nbsp;

The fact is that AI in music carries potential existential risks that we need to protect against and mitigate as best we can, and also transformational opportunities that artists and early-stage creators can use to enhance their creative process. Every player in the space has an obligation to address both: how to minimize those risks and maximize those opportunities. Simply wishing away the advent of AI music is not a strategy, and it risks leaving the future of music to players who do not prioritize and promote human artistry and the values that we prioritize and promote.

That is why we’re using this moment not just to enter into those deals, but also to pull together the various things we’re doing, both in terms of our actions on the platform and our policies, because the world is moving very fast. This is the slowest that pace of change will ever be again, and we think it’s a critical moment to reset that narrative.

Coming back to your question about these players: You can take your SoundCloud tracks, go into Moises and play with your SoundCloud content, then bring it back to the platform and publish it. Or, as a Moises user, you can create content on Moises and then publish it directly to SoundCloud. With Eleven Music, we’re the first platform where you can publish music directly from Eleven Music to SoundCloud, to an audience on a platform that has scaled global access. These are parents who share our values — which is going to drive everything we do in this space — we&#8217;re going to be artist-first.

Are these AI songs going to be eligible for the same type of royalties as the human-made creations on your platform?

We are not going to dictate to creators the ways in which they create music. What’s important to us is partnering with people who are ethically trained, who have fully licensed models and who are building their businesses through the lens of being artist-first.

UMG Inks Deal With ElevenLabs to Develop AI Music Remix&nbsp;Platform

In our history, we’ve built parts of our platform on the same sort of experimentation. Look at remixes and DJ sets. We’ve always been a safe experimental testing ground where, for years and years, creators have been able to work that way, and rights holders have been able to get paid for that derivative content. That is a big part of our lifeblood.

I think that’s one of the things driving us to speak up and try to lead this more nuanced way of thinking about AI and music. We’ve threaded that needle before.&nbsp;

I’m glad you brought up SoundCloud’s history. It is a very different platform than other companies that fall into the category of “streaming service,” and I wondered if that would mean you think of AI-created works differently.&nbsp;

We have about half a billion tracks on the platform, arguably the biggest catalog on the planet and that includes all of the content you would get at a traditional DSP. But it also includes a huge library of UGC, and that can be unsigned artists looking to get discovered, DJ sets and remixes. Where there’s monetization, rights holders, copyright owners, major labels, independent labels and independent artists are all getting paid for that listening and monetization. So I think that’s been a really important part of our story.&nbsp;

